{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/194761","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/194761/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/194761/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/194761/events","html_url":"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/194761","id":5248887671,"node_id":"I_kwDOA-j9z88AAAABONurdw","number":194761,"title":"[draft][RFC] Add the xccl2 PrcoessGroup Backend for XPU","user":{"login":"frost-intel","id":121894376,"node_id":"U_kgDOB0P16A","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/121894376?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/frost-intel","html_url":"https://github.com/frost-intel","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/frost-intel/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/frost-intel/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/frost-intel/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/frost-intel/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/frost-intel/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/frost-intel/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/frost-intel/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/frost-intel/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/frost-intel/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":679953883,"node_id":"MDU6TGFiZWw2Nzk5NTM4ODM=","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/labels/oncall:%20distributed","name":"oncall: distributed","color":"f7e101","default":false,"description":"Add this issue/PR to distributed oncall triage queue","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":10078293082,"node_id":"LA_kwDOA-j9z88AAAACWLaMWg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/labels/bot-triaged","name":"bot-triaged","color":"0b8793","default":false,"description":"This is a label only to be used by the auto triage bot","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":10694522575,"node_id":"LA_kwDOA-j9z88AAAACfXF2zw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/labels/oncall:%20distributed%20infra","name":"oncall: distributed infra","color":"71f2ec","default":false,"description":"Add this issue/PR to distributed infra oncall triage queue","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":3,"created_at":"2026-08-25T17:51:04Z","updated_at":"2026-09-18T20:14:45Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### 🚀 The feature, motivation and pitch\n\n## Summary\n\nThis RFC proposes adding `xccl2`, a `c10d::Backend` implementation for Intel XPU, which is currently part of [torchcomms](https://github.com/meta-pytorch/torchcomms/tree/main/comms/torchcomms/xccl), and the direct sibling of the in-tree `nccl2` backend. This backend is built on the oneCCL v2 C API.\n\n## Goals and Motivation\nThe primary goal and motivation of xccl2 backend bring up is to get architectural and implementation alignment for PyTorch distributed backend and its future direction to enable torchcomms backend as the default communication library for torch.distributed. While nccl2 backend is based on torchcomms implementation, if we continue to use the existing xccl backend, which is based on legacy c10d integration model, it creates a fundamental gap in distributed backend support for XPU devices from PyTorch frameworks. Given xccl support already exists in torchcomms, its adoption as xccl2 backend in the core PyTorch (similar to nccl2) is the most natural choice. \n\nThe architectural alignment to bring xccl2 backend in core Pytorch also reduces divergence between communication backends and enables xccl2 to share the same development path as other TorchComms-based integrations. Distributed features, backend abstractions, and communication capabilities can be designed, validated, and maintained within a common framework rather than requiring separate implementations that evolve independently. \n\nIn addition, an in-tree xccl2 backend strengthens core PyTorch Distributed by providing another production backend that exercises the same distributed abstractions, helping catch backend-specific assumptions, API breakages, and regressions from upstream PRs as early as possible. It also ensures stronger validation and CI coverage, broader test coverage, greater community participation, and simpler long-term maintenance. \n\nWe believe xccl2 backend provides the cleanest foundation for future distributed capabilities while keeping Intel's backend aligned with the direction of the upstream ecosystem. \n\nAdditional reasoning in the “Hosting Location” section. \n\n## Details\nPort TorchCommXCCL from TorchComms into PyTorch as xccl2 \n* For now, we keep ProcessGroupXCCL v1 which stays in torch-xpu-ops, unchanged and still the default for backend=\"xccl\". \n* No shared base, no changes to nccl2. \n* Does not include symmetric memory, windows, reconfigure or shrink functionality. Absent from the torchcomms implementation as well. \n* No new c10d interfaces. xccl2 implements the existing Backend surface; the only new binding is a read-only backend_version property, because c10d has no virtual for it. \n\n## Landing plan\n\nThe change is < 6,000 lines under torch/csrc/distributed/c10d/xccl2/, to be landed as eight commits. Like the nccl2 porting from torchcomms, we use agents to port code. The changes are in the eight commits, each independently reviewable, in dependency order: \n\n| # | Commit | What it adds |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Add backend-agnostic TorchComms helpers | vendored infra (`Batch`, `Logging`, `TracingGuard`) |\n| 2 | Port the XcclApi oneCCL abstraction | the whole vendor surface |\n| 3 | Port the work object, work queue and bootstrap | `WorkXCCL`, queue, Store rendezvous |\n| 4 | Port the ProcessGroupXCCL declaration and engine helpers | header + utils |\n| 5 | Port the XCCL engine implementation | collective bodies |\n| 6 | Implement the `c10d::Backend` surface | the overrides c10d calls |\n| 7 | Wire into build, Python bindings and backend registry | `build_variables.bzl`, `init.cpp`, `distributed_c10d.py`, cmake |\n| 8 | Enroll in the shared c10d backend test suites | `BackendConfig` entry + unit tests |\n\nCommits 1–6 add files only. The first change to an existing file is commit 7.\n\nBuilding the code is gated behind `USE_C10D_XCCL`, as the existing ProcessGroupXCCL backend is. This change initially touches zero files under nccl2/. It ships with its own unit tests and enrolls in the shared c10d backend suites. \n\n## Validation\n1. **New Unit tests.** `test/distributed/test_c10d_xccl2.py` mirrors `test_c10d_nccl2.py` (5 tests).\n2. **Other xccl2-specific c10d tests.** A single `BackendConfig` entry enrols `xccl2` in the four suites driven off `C10D_BACKENDS` (collectives, p2p, process_group, nan_check).  `gloo` and the `nccl` backends are unaffected.\n3. Full set of distributed c10d tests using `xccl2`.\n \n## Hosting location\n`xccl2` is proposed for the PyTorch tree rather than `torch-xpu-ops`, where the v1 ProcessGroupXCCL lives. This is for the following reasons:\n- **Streamline the merge process.** When breaking changes occur in PyTorch (whether in Backend, c10d, etc) and a fix requires changes in `torch-xpu-ops`, this requires 2 (or more) separate PRs which can cause significant delays. For example, update PyTorch with new feature, then update torch-xpu-ops to use new feature and pass XPU CI, then update pin in PyTorch for final merge.\n- **A ProcessGroup is not an operator.** `torch-xpu-ops` is focused on ATen kernels, which have a stable, versioned boundary: the operator schema. `c10d::Backend` is a C++ ABI with no stability guarantee, and `xccl2` integrates with the distributed tools (flight recorder, trace hooks, `Store` rendezvous, watchdog machinery, etc). \n  - If at some point we use custom communication kernels outside of oneCCL (similar to ncclx), we would add these in `torch-xpu-ops`. However, there are no current plans to do this, and it remains highly unlikely.\n- **Interface changes break out-of-tree backends silently.** In-tree, a breaking change fails to compile in the PR that causes it. Behind a pin, it breaks later, for someone else, disconnected from the cause — and is invisible to CI until the SHA moves.  In-tree, `xccl2` compiles on every PR touching c10d even on machines with no XPU.\n- **Reduce drift between backends** `xccl2` and `nccl2` are two ports of one engine with near 1:1 file correspondence and two files byte-identical after renaming.\n\n### Counterarguments\n\n- **\"v1 lives in torch-xpu-ops, so v2 should too.\"** The strongest objection, and it is about consistency rather than correctness. At the time ProcessGroupXCCL v1 was written, there were significant gaps in API and functionality between NCCL and oneCCL. Currently differences in functionality are much smaller, and the API is almost identical now.\n- **\"Intel wants to iterate without PyTorch review latency.\"** Legitimate. However, this will likely increase code duplication. In addition, ProcessGroupXCCL and XCCL2 are far more stable than even a few months ago.\n- **\"It bloats non-XPU builds.\"** It does not; the sources are gated and contribute nothing when XCCL is not built.\n\n## Long-term Plan\nOur preliminary plan to maintain xccl2 is listed as follows: \n* Minor updates and bugfixes for xccl2 \n  * Add recent functional updates made to nccl2 into xccl2, where appropriate \n  * Ensure feature parity and UT equivalence between xccl and xccl2. \n* Deprecate and eventually remove ProcessGroupXCCL in torch-xpu-ops \n  * Adopt xccl2 as default backend for XPU \n\nAdditionally, we can collaborate with Meta and PyTorch maintainers to design and define shared abstraction in order to remove code duplication and have a unified common APIs for future distributed backend. This is our initial proposal for nccl2 and xccl2 backends: \n\n* Refactor nccl2 and xccl2 to share a common c10d::Backend with common implementations to reduce code duplication. \n  * No observable behavior change \n  * Uses the underly vendor API shims (XcclApi/NcclApi) as integration point for common backend implementations \n  * Begin with xccl2, then touch nccl2 to use the common implementations. \n  * xccl2 overrides the parent implementations where necessary \n  * Additional details to be provided in a follow-up RFC \n\ncc @awgu @wanchaol @fegin @fduwjj @wz337 @wconstab @d4l3k @pragupta @msaroufim @dcci @aditvenk @weifengpy @kapilsh","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/194761/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/194761/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null}